  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 206 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFriday...A more unsettled pattern will arrive by late week and break \nthe brief heat wave across the area. Shortwave ridging will depart \nas broad troughing settles south across the Western CONUS. As a more \npronounced disturbance crosses central Canada, an attendant cool \nfront will settle south into the Central Plains. Through Friday, \nthis feature will slowly translate south and east into western and \ncentral Nebraska. Ample moist southerly flow will feed into this and \nshould allow for thunderstorm development during the afternoon. The \nStorm Prediction Center maintains a Marginal Risk severe weather \noutlook (Level 1 of 5) for most areas east of Highway 83. Overall, \nthis will come down to placement of the frontal boundary at the time \nof storm development. Typically, models tend to under-forecast \nprogression of a frontal boundary with ample cold air advection \nbehind it. Because of this, confidence remains relatively low with \nrespect to thunderstorm coverage across the area. Folks should \ncontinue to monitor later forecasts regarding precise locations \nunder the threat for severe thunderstorms.\n\nSaturday and beyond...flow aloft will gradually transition to more \nsouthwesterly by early next week. This will promote increasing \nmoisture aloft as an influx of moisture from the eastern Pacific \nwill arrive. PWATs will again climb with values progged to exceed \n1.0\" or nearly 2-3 sigma for mid-September. Temperatures will waver \nday to day as recurring rounds of rain and thunderstorms dot the \nforecast period. NBM inner-quartile spread remains fairly large, \nparticularly Sunday and beyond, thus confidence is low in precise \ndaytime temperatures. Widespread wetting rains appear possible late \nSaturday night into Sunday where the NBM shows 50%+ probabilities of \nseeing > 0.25\" rainfall for areas north of Interstate 80. This will \nlikely be refined some in the coming days as smaller scale details \nbecome more certain so stay tuned. Broad upper ridging will become \nestablished across the lower Mississippi valley towards the middle \nof next week. This should allow fairly enhanced mid-level flow to \npersist and with it, regular precipitation chances. The Climate \nPrediction Center shows this similar thinking with 40-50% chance of \nseeing above normal precipitation for the September 14-18 period \n(Monday through Friday). NBM temperature spreads remain large, \nincreasing to 20F by late week, so it remains to be seen whether we \nsee continued above normal temperatures or perhaps a return to more \nseasonable levels.",
